[38;2;124;124;124mcounterexample works regardless.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me also consider: could the "canonical pre-order topology" be the topology [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mgenerated by the sets $\{y : x \leq y\}$ for all $x$ (i.e., the upper sets as a [0m[38;2;124;124;124mbasis)? That's the Alexandrov topology. Or could it be the topology generated by[0m[38;2;124;124;124mboth upper and lower sets? [0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIn any case, for my finite example, any reasonable topology derived from the [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mpre-order will make all singletons Borel (since the pre-order is a partial order[0m[38;2;124;124;124mon a finite set, and the interval topology on a finite poset is discrete). So [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthe Borel σ-algebra is the full power set, and the counterexample works.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me write the proof now.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mActually, I want to present this more carefully. Let me think about the cleanest[0m[38;2;124;124;124mway to present the counterexample.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe key insight is: in a monoid where translation by some element is non-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124minjective (collapses the whole space to a proper subset), a finite (or σ-finite)[0m[38;2;124;124;124mtranslation-invariant measure must be zero.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSpecifically, if there exists $a \in T$ such that $a + T \subsetneq T$ (i.e., [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$a$ is not invertible and $a + T \neq T$), then for any finite translation-[0m
[38;2;124;124;124minvariant measure $\mu$:[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$\mu(a + T) = \mu(T)$$[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m$$\mu(T \setminus (a + T)) = \mu(T) - \mu(a + T) = 0$$[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mAnd if we can propagate this to show all singletons have measure 0, we're done.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIn my example, $a + T = \{a, b\} \subsetneq T = \{0, a, b\}$, and $T \setminus [0m
[38;2;124;124;124m(a + T) = \{0\}$. So $\mu(\{0\}) = 0$. Then $a + \{0\} = \{a\}$ gives $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\mu(\{a\}) = 0$, and $a + \{a\} = \{b\}$ gives $\mu(\{b\}) = 0$.[0m

